---
import LabAssessment from "../../components/LabAssessment.astro";
import { MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_PRESSAO } from "@ovh/otel-domain";
import { PressaoModos } from "../../components/PressaoModos.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/ModosDoLab.css";
import "../../components/CpuVitrine.css";
import "../../components/ContextoLab.css";
import "../../components/ImplantacaoLab.css";
import "../../components/MetricaLab.css";
import "@ovh/motion/src/react/motion.css";
import "@ovh/stage3d/src/iso.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
---

<Base
  domain="otel"
  title="Backpressure and drops — OpenLabs"
  description="When the backend goes down, the Collector's exporter queues and retries with backoff — until the queue fills, the retries give up, the memory_limiter refuses at the edge, or, without a limiter, the process runs out of memory and loses the whole queue."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel")}>OpenTelemetry Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>Where spans wait when the backend is down, and where they die</h1>
        <p>
          The backend was down for twelve seconds. Someone raised
          <code>sending_queue.queue_size</code> after the last incident so
          &ldquo;nothing gets lost&rdquo;. This time the Collector restarted
          with an out-of-memory kill, and the traces from those twelve seconds
          are gone — along with the ones that were queued before.
        </p>
        <p>
          A span can die in four places on the way out: refused by the
          <code>memory_limiter</code>, dropped because the queue is full,
          dropped because the retries gave up, or lost with the process.
          Only the first one is something the application ever hears about.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>In 3D the queue is a column.</p>
        <p>Height is occupancy; red is past a limit.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-palco">
      <PressaoModos client:only="react" />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Run a few ticks, then take the backend down.</b> The queue climbs
            one batch a tick, and the retries spread out: 1, 2, 4 ticks.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Keep it down with queue_size 4.</b> New batches are dropped as
            &ldquo;queue full&rdquo;, and the oldest give up after their retries
            run out.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Switch to queue_size 15 and do it again.</b> With the limiter,
            batches are refused at the edge before memory runs out. Turn the
            limiter off: the queue grows past the process's memory and the
            crash takes everything queued with it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Bring the backend back.</b> The queue drains a couple of batches
            a tick, not all at once — recovery takes time too.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>Answer first, then try it in the simulation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="A Collector has no memory_limiter and a sending_queue larger than the memory it can use. The backend goes down for longer than usual. What happens to the queued spans?"
          opcoes={[
            "The process runs out of memory and the in-memory queue is lost with it",
            "They wait in the queue until the backend returns, however long that takes",
            "The exporter refuses new spans and the SDK gets an error",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="The sending queue is held in process memory unless a persistent storage extension is configured. Without a memory_limiter, nothing stops it from growing past what the process can hold, so a long outage ends in an out-of-memory crash. The refusal back to the SDK is the memory_limiter's job — which is why it goes first in the pipeline."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people get wrong</h2>
        <p>Each row is a belief the simulation is built to take apart. The right column is the documentation, with the link.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-mal-entendidos">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What people believe</th>
              <th scope="col">What the docs say</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_PRESSAO.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{m.crenca}</td>
                  <td>
                    {m.spec}{" "}
                    <a href={m.fonte} rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">
                      source
                    </a>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Where this connects</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={url("labs/collector-pipeline")}>The Collector pipeline</a> is
          where <code>memory_limiter</code> and the exporter sit, and why the
          order of processors matters. <a href={url("labs/agent-or-gateway")}>Agent
          or gateway</a> is where this queue lives — on every node, or in a few
          gateways — and so how much one outage costs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <LabAssessment href="labs/backpressure-and-drops" />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .lab-palco {
    margin-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos th,
  .lab-mal-entendidos td {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
